FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:800 — New frame/scope arms for `Unless`, `While`, `Repeat`/`Until`, `Module`, `Test` and `Try` (also src/lsp.rs:810, src/lsp.rs:874, src/lsp.rs:896, src/lsp.rs:897) ship without a single navigation test. An unbalanced `Frame`/scope in any of them silently corrupts every later `goto_definition`/`find_references`/`completions` answer at that point in the file (the scope never pops, or an inner `end` pops the wrong frame). The REPORT's "nesting_recursion" row only names `for`, object and function scopes, so this is a new branch with no test on either side. Fix: add `unless`/`while`/`repeat…until`/`module`/`test`/`try…catch` fixtures asserting a binding declared in the block does not leak past its terminator and that goto still resolves after it. (Read at src/lsp.rs:800-928; not executed, read-only.)
2. [MAJOR] tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:978 — `navigation_sample(lines: usize) -> Duration` plus `edge_navigation_handles_five_thousand_lines_quickly` (tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:1018) make a wall-clock assertion. The REPORT describes it as comparing 1000 vs 5000 lines and "asserts linear scaling", which is a timing comparison; on a loaded/slow CI runner the ratio is nondeterministic and can fail a green gate intermittently (hunt list #4). The "resource_limit" matrix row is claimed covered by this, but a timing ratio is not a resource bound. Fix: assert a deterministic property (e.g., count `Lexer::tokenize`/scope operations, or a generous absolute ceiling such as no more than N seconds) instead of a ratio between two wall-clock samples. I did not read the assertion body (tool budget), so this is unverified beyond the REPORT's own description of it.
3. [MINOR] tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:1577 — `edge_builtin_references_exclude_member_and_type_names` calls `list` a builtin ("say list" ... "not a use of the builtin"), but `list` is not in `BUILTIN_NAMES` (src/stdlib.rs:2082-2161 lists `to_list`/`list_map`, not `list`). The test therefore exercises the *unresolved-name* path, not a real builtin, so the asserted claim about builtin references is misleading; the real builtin-with-type-collision case remains untested. Fix: use an actual member of `BUILTIN_NAMES` spelled identically to a type name (or change the wording and add a genuine builtin fixture).

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
